{%- set churchIntro -%}<p class="page-intro">St Peter's stands in Abbots Morton, a small village just north of the Lenches, and is the A in the ARCH Benefice. The village was once a country retreat for the abbots of Evesham, and the moated site of their manor house lies close to the church. St Peter's is listed Grade I.</p>{%- endset -%}
{%- set churchAbout -%}<h2>A short history</h2>
    <p>The church stands on the site of a Saxon one, and the oldest walls of today's building date from the 12th century. In the mid-1300s the chancel was rebuilt to the full width of the nave and a chapel was added on the north side, now used for the organ and choir. The tower followed around 1400, and the timber-framed porch in the early 1400s. The list of rectors goes back to Petrus le Meare in 1288.</p>
    <h2>What to look out for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The 15th-century roof, whose beams are often little more than roughly shaped tree branches. The village raised over £1,300 in 1955 to save them.</li>
      <li>Two 15th-century pews in the porch. The south door is now the only way in.</li>
      <li>The bells. The tenor is a 15th-century "Royal Head" bell, its inscription marked with the heads of Edward III and Queen Philippa; another is dated 1633, and two were cast by Thomas Mears of London in 1842.</li>
      <li>The tower, restored in 2015 with Chicksgrove stone after a £45,000 appeal, with new oak louvres made in the workshop at Gloucester Cathedral.</li>
    </ul>{%- endset -%}
